Title:
SILVERPICK — Windows User-Mode Shellcode Development Framework (WUMSDF)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
SILVERPICK is a Windows x64 user-mode framework for building position-independent shellcode (PIC) “blobs” in C/C++ with a MinGW-w64 + GNU Make toolchain. It focuses on reducing typical shellcode development friction (stack alignment, section ordering, CRT avoidance, and dependency-less API resolution) while keeping output small and PIC-safe. The repo demonstrates using GCC function attributes to auto-generate a 16-byte stack alignment stub (avoiding hand-written assembly) and to force entrypoint ordering via a `.init` section rather than linker scripts. It includes robust “stack string” construction resistant to compiler optimizations, compile-time and run-time API hashing (64-bit FNV-1a with modified parameters), and minimal CRT-like memory primitives using string instructions/intrinsics. It also provides custom GetModuleHandle/GetProcAddress-style routines via PEB walking and PE export parsing, wrapped in macros for runtime dynamic linking. Useful primarily for red team capability developers, shellcode/implant authors, and researchers studying PIC tradeoffs and detection surfaces (including an errata noting a brittle Defender signature on the sample).

Technical Focus:
- Windows x64 position-independent code (PIC) in C/C++
- GCC function attributes for stack alignment and section placement (`force_align_arg_pointer`, `section(".init")`)
- Stack-string generation via C++ template metaprogramming
- API hashing (64-bit FNV-1a, compile-time `consteval`, modified offset basis)
- CRT-less memory routines using x86 string instructions (`rep movsb/stosb`, inline asm)
- PEB/module enumeration and PE export parsing for dependency-less API resolution

Use Cases:
- Build Windows x64 shellcode payloads in C/C++ without relying on the CRT/import table
- Create PIC loaders/stagers that resolve APIs dynamically via hashes
- Generate OPSEC-friendly small binaries with controlled code layout and disassembly artifacts
- Research/diff compiler/linker behaviors affecting shellcode correctness and size
- Prototype polymorphic API-hash strategies to reduce static signature reliability

Keywords:
Windows x64, shellcode, PIC, position-independent code, MinGW-w64, GCC, GNU Make, MSYS2, force_align_arg_pointer, section attribute, .init section, PEB walking, GetModuleHandle replacement, GetProcAddress replacement, PE export parsing, FNV-1a 64-bit, consteval, API hashing, stack strings, rep movsb, rep stosb, inline assembly, dynamic API resolution, OPSEC-friendly build flags, Microsoft Defender signature, Stardust, WILDBEAST